    {
      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this certificate carefully.\n\nThis is an OIML Certificate of Conformity from the Czech Metrology Institute (CMI):\n- Certificate No.: R49/2006-CZ-12.01\n- Recommendation: R 49, edition 2006, accuracy class 2\n- Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, CZ\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Applicant: ELIS PLZEŇ a.s., Luční 15, P.O.BOX 126, 304 26 Plzeň, Czech Republic\n- Manufacturer: ELIS PLZEŇ a.s. (same address)\n- Certified type: Ultrasound Water Meter, Types: FLOMIC FL5024 and FLOMIC FL5044\n- Test report: No. 6015-PT-P0008-10, 39 pages (plus annexes)\n- Date issued: 13 April 2012\n- Pages: 5\n\nModel family: two models:\n- FLOMIC FL5024 (single-beam): DN 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200\n- FLOMIC FL5044 (double-beam): DN 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200\n\nSo this is a model × DN (nominal diameter) matrix. The best representation: models identified by type + DN, e.g. \"FL5024 DN32\", \"FL5024 DN40\", etc.\n\nLet me structure:\n\nFL5024 table (page 3): DN 32, 40, 50, 65, 80\n- Overload flowrate Q4: ≤ 12.5, ≤ 20.0, ≤ 31.3, ≤ 50.0, ≤ 78.8 m³/h\n- Permanent flowrate Q3 (footnote 1): ≤ 10, ≤ 16, ≤ 25, ≤ 40, ≤ 63 m³/h\n- Transitional flowrate Q2: ≥ 0.32, ≥ 0.51, ≥ 0.80, ≥ 1.28, ≥ 2.02 m³/h\n- Minimum flowrate Q1: ≥ 0.20, ≥ 0.32, ≥ 0.50, ≥ 0.80, ≥ 1.26 m³/h\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: ≤ 50 (footnote 2) for DN32-80... wait, colspan=4 covers first four columns (32,40,50,65), and ≤ 40 (footnote 2) for DN80. Wait let me re-check.\n\nRow: `<tr><td>Ratio Q3 / Q1</td><td colspan=\"4\">≤ 50 <sup>2</sup></td><td>≤ 40 <sup>2</sup></td></tr>`\nColumns: DN 32, 40, 50, 65, 80. So colspan 4 covers DN32, 40, 50, 65 with ≤ 50, and DN80 with ≤ 40.\n\nSecond table (page 4) — this is a continuation of FL5024 (page 3 said \"Ultrasound Water Meter FLOMIC FL5024\" then table with DN 32-80; page 4 has no header but continues with DN 100, 125, 150, 200). Yes, page 4 continues FL5024 for larger DN:\n- Q4: ≤ 125.0, ≤ 200.0, ≤ 312.5, ≤ 500.0\n- Q3 (fn 1): ≤ 100, ≤ 160, ≤ 250, ≤ 400\n- Q2: ≥ 3.20, ≥ 5.12, ≥ 8.00, ≥ 12.80\n- Q1: ≥ 2.00, ≥ 3.20, ≥ 5.00, ≥ 8.00\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: ≤ 50 (fn 2) for all\n- Ratio Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Ratio Q4/Q3: 1.25\n- Orientation: arbitrary\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- MPE_l: ± 5 %\n- MPE_u: ± 2 % for water with temperature ≤ 30 °C\n- Temperature class: T30\n- Water pressure classes: MAP 16\n- Pressure-loss classes: ΔP 25\n- Indicating range: 999 999 to 9 999 999 m³ (for DN100-200 page 4; page 3 was 99 999 to 999 999)\n- Resolution of indicating device [L]: 10 (page 4); page 3: 1 (DN32-80) except DN80 is 10? Let me re-check: `<td>Resolution of the indicating device [L]</td><td colspan=\"4\">1</td><td>10</td>` — colspan 4 covers DN32,40,50,65 with 1, and DN80 with 10.\n- Resolution of rapid testing [pulse/L]: 1.6, 1.6, 0.64, 0.32, 0.32 (page 3); 0.16, 0.16, 0.16, 0.064 (page 4)\n- Flow profile sensitivity: U10S D5 (DN32), U10 D5 (DN40-80); page 4: U10 D5 all\n- Length [mm]: 260, 300, 300, 300, 350 (page 3); 350 all (page 4)\n- Connection: standard flange\n- Environmental class: B\n- Electromagnetic class: E1 and E2\n- Software: M16V400 – FL50X4\n- W&M checksum: B543DAA9\n- Power supply: 3.6 V / 19 Ah\n- Battery life: 8 years\n\nFL5044 table (page 5): DN 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200\n- Q4: ≤ 50, ≤ 79, ≤ 125, ≤ 200, ≤ 313, ≤ 500\n- Q3 (fn 1): ≤ 40, ≤ 63, ≤ 100, ≤ 160, ≤ 250, ≤ 400\n- Q2: ≥ 0.64, ≥ 2.50, ≥ 2.50, ≥ 2.56, ≥ 4.00, ≥ 6.40\n- Q1: ≥ 0.40, ≥ 1.58, ≥ 1.59, ≥ 1.60, ≥ 2.50, ≥ 4.00\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: ≤ 100, ≤ 40, ≤ 63, ≤ 100, ≤ 100, ≤ 100 (all fn 2)\n- Ratio Q2/Q1: 1.6\n- Ratio Q4/Q3: 1.25\n- Orientation: arbitrary\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- MPE_l: ± 5 %\n- MPE_u: ± 2 % for ≤ 30 °C\n- Temp class: T30\n- MAP 16\n- ΔP 25\n- Indicating range: 999 999 to 9 999 999 m³\n- Resolution indicating device: 1 (DN65), 10 (rest)\n- Rapid testing resolution: 0.32, 0.32, 0.16, 0.16, 0.16, 0.064\n- Flow profile: U5 D3\n- Length: 300 (DN65), 350 (rest)\n- Connection: Standard flange\n- Environmental: B\n- EMC: E1 and E2\n- Software: M16V400 – FL50X4\n- Checksum: B543DAA9\n- Power: 3.6 V / 19 Ah\n- Battery life: 8 years\n\nSo models: I'll define model_id as \"FL5024 DN32\", \"FL5024 DN40\", ..., \"FL5024 DN200\", \"FL5044 DN65\", ..., \"FL5044 DN200\". That's 9 + 6 = 15 models.\n\nActually, maybe better structure: model_family with family FLOMIC FL50X4? The certificate type designations: FLOMIC FL5024 and FLOMIC FL5044. I'll set model_family.family_name = \"FLOMIC FL50X4\" (from the software version)? Hmm, that's speculative. The software version says \"M16V400 – FL50X4\" which suggests the family. But to avoid fabrication, maybe family_name null. Actually \"FL50X4\" is a wildcard pattern mentioned in the software version. I could set family_name to null to be safe, or \"FLOMIC FL50X4\". I think it's reasonable to leave null since no explicit family name is given. Actually the schema says \"explicit family name if any, else null\". There's no explicit family name. So null.\n\nModels list: 15 entries with attributes per model:\n- model_id: \"FL5024-DN32\" etc.\n- label: \"FLOMIC FL5024, DN 32\"\n- attributes: could include beam count? FL5024 = single-beam, FL5044 = double-beam. That's stated in description. I could add attributes: {\"beam_configuration\": \"single-beam\"} / {\"beam_configuration\": \"double-beam\"}.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level entries — attribute × model matrix. Attributes that vary:\n- nominal_diameter (mm): values per model\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h): upper bounds — values with max (≤ means max). Hmm, \"≤ 12.5\" means max 12.5. So value {min: null, max: 12.5}.\n- permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h): max, footnote 1)\n- transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h): min (≥), value {min: X, max: null}\n- minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h): min\n- ratio_q3_q1: max, footnote 2)\n- resolution_of_indicating_device (L)\n- resolution_of_rapid_testing_device (pulse/L)\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: string \"U10S D5\" / \"U10 D5\" / \"U5 D3\"\n- length (mm)\n\nConstant attributes → type_level:\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless)\n- ratio_q4_q3: 1.25\n- orientation_limitation: \"arbitrary\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mpe_lower_zone: ±5 % → value \"±5\"? Rules say numbers not strings. ±5% → value 5? Sign matters. Hmm. MPE ±5 % — I could store value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\", and maybe a label mpe_lower_zone_percent with \"±\". The structured value shape allows string values too. \"±5 %\" is kind of a signed range. I think value: {min: -5, max: 5}, unit_symbol: \"%\" is clean. Actually MPE is a maximum permissible error magnitude ±5%. I'll use value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\", attribute name mpe_lower_zone (max absolute). Hmm. Alternatively value: \"±5\". Rules prefer numbers. I'll do {min: -5, max: 5}? That could be misinterpreted. Let me think: the rule about ranges: \"-10°C/+40°C\" becomes {min,max}. \"± 5 %\" symmetric — a range from -5 to 5. I'll go with {min: -5, max: 5}, unit \"%\". Similarly MPE_u ±2 %. But the MPE_u also has condition \"for water with temperature ≤ 30 °C\". That's a config_level maybe: attribute mpe_upper_zone with condition. Actually I can capture as type_level mpe_upper_zone: value 2, unit \"%\", plus separate note. Hmm, the condition \"for water with temperature ≤ 30 °C\" is important. I could use config_level:\n- attribute: mpe_upper_zone, axis: water_temperature, values: [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}]. That works nicely per rule 5.\n\nActually simpler: type_level mpe_upper_zone: {value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"} and a footnote or description. But config_level is designed for this. Let me use config_level with axis \"water_temperature\".\n\n- temperature_class: \"T30\" (string)\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP 16\" (string)\n- pressure_loss_class: \"ΔP 25\" → canonical label pressure_loss_class, value \"25\"? The class is ΔP25. I'll store value \"ΔP25\" as string or value 25 with the ΔP implied in attribute name. I'll do attribute pressure_loss_class, value \"ΔP25\" string. Hmm rules: no LaTeX, no footnote markers; strings allowed for symbols. \"ΔP25\" fine.\n- indicating_range: varies! Page 3 (DN32-80): 99 999 to 999 999 m³; page 4 (DN100-200) and FL5044: 999 999 to 9 999 999 m³. So indicating range is model_level. Values like {min: 99999, max: 999999}.\n\nWait, \"99 999 to 999 999\" — with space as thousands separator: 99 999 = 99999. So {min: 99999, max: 999999} and {min: 999999, max: 9999999}.\n\n- connection_type: \"standard flange\"\n- environmental_class: \"B\"\n- electromagnetic_class: \"E1 and E2\" → maybe two values? \"E1 and E2\" string fine.\n- software_version: \"M16V400 – FL50X4\" → string \"M16V400-FL50X4\"\n- wm_checksum: \"B543DAA9\"\n- power_supply_voltage: 3.6 V; power_supply_battery_capacity: 19 Ah (from \"3.6 V / 19 Ah\"). Split into two: power_supply_voltage value 3.6 unit V; battery_capacity value 19 unit Ah.\n- battery_effective_life: 8 years.\n\nmatrix_tables: I should preserve the three tables. Table names: \"Ultrasound Water Meter FLOMIC FL5024 (DN 32–80)\", \"FLOMIC FL5024 (DN 100–200)\", \"FLOMIC FL5044 (DN 65–200)\". Columns with units. Rows per DN.\n\nAlternatively, one matrix_table per physical table. Yes, preserve them as matrix_tables with rows as objects keyed by canonical column names.\n\nFootnotes:\n- 1) The value of Q3 shall be chosen from the R5 line of ISO 3:1973.\n- 2) The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen from the R10 line from ISO 3:1973 and this value shall be higher than 10.\n\nFootnote markers: \"1)\" and \"2)\". The markdown shows superscript `<sup>1</sup>` and `<sup>2</sup>`. I'll record markers as \"1)\" and \"2)\" following the example in the schema (\"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"]).\n\nCertificate fields:\n- number: \"R49/2006-CZ-12.01\"\n- scheme: The OIML-CS scheme... In 2006-2012, certificates were under the OIML Certificate System (not \"basic\" vs \"MAA\"). Schema says scheme (A|B). Hmm. OIML-CS has Scheme A (basic) and Scheme B (MAA). This cert predates MAA; it's a standard OIML certificate. Recommendation R49 MAA exists but this cert doesn't mention MAA. I'll use \"A\" since it's the basic system. Actually, careful: rule says don't fabricate. The schema demands scheme (A|B). Standard certificates without MAA are Scheme A. I'll put \"A\".\n- project_number: not present. Omit.\n- page_total: 5\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"2012-04-13\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present explicitly. Omit? The issuing authority is CMI. There's no issuer ID number given. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Czech Metrology Institute\"\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"CZ\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\"\n- person_title: not given for Jan Kalandra (he signs as \"The Issuing Authority\"). Actually the signature block: \"The Issuing Authority — Jan Kalandra\" and \"The CIML Member — Pavel Klenovský\". So person_title could be \"The Issuing Authority\" for Kalandra. I'll set person_title: \"Issuing Authority\". Hmm, but there are two persons. The schema has one person_responsible. I'll keep Jan Kalandra as person_responsible with title \"The Issuing Authority\". Pavel Klenovský is CIML Member — no field for that; could skip or include in... there's no field. Skip. Actually maybe I can note in nothing. Fine.\n- phone, email, website: not present. Omit.\n- oiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\napplicants[]: ELIS PLZEŇ a.s., address lines: [\"Luční 15\", \"P.O.BOX 126\", \"304 26 Plzeň\", \"Czech Republic\"]\n\nmanufacturers[]: same.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\". The schema examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\"... For water meter, category \"Water meter\". The doc calls it \"Ultrasound Water Meter\". Category: \"Water meter\" seems canonical; description says intended for metering cold potable water. I'll use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"FLOMIC FL5024\", \"FLOMIC FL5044\"]\n- module_designation: null (not applicable/not stated). For water meters under R49, modules... not mentioned. Omit or null. I'll omit? Schema shows the field; rule 8 says omit if not present. I'll omit module_designation. Hmm, but schema lists it. Safer to omit since not present.\n- description: the measuring system description text (condensed English).\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null\n- models[]: 15 entries.\n\nFor each model: model_id \"FL5024-DN32\" ... label \"FLOMIC FL5024 DN 32\". attributes: {beam: \"single-beam\"} for FL5024, \"double-beam\" for FL5044; maybe nominal_diameter 32. Attributes within models{} could hold static per-model info like beam configuration.\n\ncomponents[]: The description mentions the water meters consist of flow sensor and measuring transducer with electronic calculator. Should I create components? Rule 6 says include only if cert names sub-assemblies. The description does name \"flow sensor\" and \"measuring transducer with the electronic calculator\". These are described generically without type designations. I could add:\n- {role: \"flow_sensor\", type_designations: [], ...} hmm type_designations empty. Maybe include with description in characteristics? The schema: components: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. I could add:\n- role: \"flow sensor\", type_designations: [] (integrated, compact), characteristics: {body_material: \"ductile iron\", ...}\n- role: \"measuring transducer / calculator\", characteristics: {housing: \"aluminum box with plastic lid\", display: \"LCD\", interfaces: [\"RS232\", \"impulse output\", \"current output\"], max_communication_cable_length: 2 m}\n\nThis adds value. I'll include components with characteristics as structured values? The components characteristics{} shape isn't strictly specified (the strict structured-value rule applies to characteristic values). I'll apply structured values there too where sensible. Keep it modest.\n\nActually, let me be careful not to overcomplicate. I'll include two components:\n1. role: \"flow_sensor\", type_designations: [] — but empty array is weird. The flow sensor has no separate type designation. Maybe include with a note. I'll include characteristics: {measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\", beam_configuration per model...}. Hmm, beam config is model-level.\n\nAlternatively skip components since no type designations. But the rule says \"include ONLY if cert names sub-assemblies\" — it does name them (\"flow sensor\", \"measuring transducer with the electronic calculator\"). I'll include them minimally:\n\ncomponents: [\n  {role: \"flow_sensor\", type_designations: [], alternatives: null, characteristics: {body_material: {value: \"ductile iron\"}, connection: {value: \"standard flange\"}, sensors_note...}},\n  {role: \"measuring_transducer_with_calculator\", type_designations: [], characteristics: {housing: ..., display: \"LCD\", communication_interfaces: [\"RS232\", \"impulse output\", \"current output\"], max_communication_cable_length: {value: 2, unit_symbol: \"m\"}, power_supply: battery}}\n]\n\nHmm, type_designations empty arrays... Rule 8: don't fabricate; empty is fine as \"not applicable\". I might just omit type_designations key for those. The schema lists it but omitting is acceptable.\n\nAlso FL5024 DN32 requires cross-type flow straightener — that's a config-level or note. I can add to description and maybe a type_level characteristic: flow_straightener_required: \"cross type (FL5024 DN32)\". Better: config_level entry? It's a requirement for a specific model. I could add model-level note in that model's attributes: {\"flow_straightener\": \"cross type required\"}. I'll add to the FL5024-DN32 model attributes.\n\nAlso FL5024 = single-beam (one pair of sensors), FL5044 = double-beam (two pairs). Add attributes beam_configuration.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level:\n- ratio_q2_q1: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: null}\n- ratio_q4_q3: {value: 1.25}\n- orientation_limitation: \"arbitrary\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — with note it's ±5%. Maybe value {min:-5, max:5}. I'll use {min: -5, max: 5} unit \"%\" — hmm. Actually simpler and less ambiguous: value 5 with attribute name mpe_lower_zone_max... The label \"maximum permissible error\" implies magnitude. I'll go with value: 5, unit \"%\", attribute \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\". Add attribute mpe_upper in config_level. For consistency maybe both in config? MPE_l has no condition. Keep MPE_l in type_level as 5 %.\n\nHmm, but ± matters. Let me store value: {min: -5, max: 5}, unit \"%\"? The example in rules: `+5°C/+40°C` → {min:5, max:40}. So signs get absorbed into min/max. \"±5%\" → min -5, max 5. That seems consistent. I'll do that for MPEs.\n\n- temperature_class: {value: \"T30\"}\n- water_pressure_class: {value: \"MAP 16\"}\n- pressure_loss_class: {value: \"ΔP25\"} — canonical: value \"ΔP25\". Or attribute pressure_loss_class value \"25\" with unit kPa? ΔP25 means max pressure loss 25 kPa at Q3. But cert states class ΔP 25. I'll record value \"ΔP25\". Hmm, could do both: attribute pressure_loss_class value \"ΔP25\". Fine.\n- connection_type: {value: \"standard flange\"}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"B\"}\n- electromagnetic_class: {value: \"E1 and E2\"} — maybe [\"E1\",\"E2\"]? Value must be number/obj/string. I'll do \"E1, E2\"? Keep \"E1 and E2\".\n- software_version: {value: \"M16V400 – FL50X4\"} → \"M16V400-FL50X4\"\n- wm_checksum: {value: \"B543DAA9\"}\n- power_supply_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_capacity: {value: 19, unit_symbol: \"Ah\"}\n- battery_effective_life: {value: 8, unit_symbol: \"years\"} — unit \"a\" (annum)? Use \"years\".\n\ncharacteristics.config_level:\n- attribute: \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\", axis: \"water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: {min:-2, max:2}, unit_symbol: \"%\"}]. Hmm condition string \"≤ 30 °C\" contains unit; acceptable in condition. Or condition: \"water_temperature ≤ 30\". I'll write condition \"≤ 30 °C\".\n\ncharacteristics.model_level entries: (attribute, unit_symbol, values [{model, value, footnote_markers}])\n\n1. nominal_diameter, unit mm: FL5024-DN32:32, DN40:40, DN50:50, DN65:65, DN80:80, DN100:100, DN125:125, DN150:150, DN200:200; FL5044-DN65:65, DN80:80, DN100:100, DN125:125, DN150:150, DN200:200.\n\n2. overload_flowrate_q4, m³/h, max values:\nFL5024: 12.5, 20.0, 31.3, 50.0, 78.8, 125.0, 200.0, 312.5, 500.0\nFL5044: 50, 79, 125, 200, 313, 500\nEach as {min: null, max: X}.\n\n3. permanent_flowrate_q3, m³/h, max, footnote 1) on all:\nFL5024: 10, 16, 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400\nFL5044: 40, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400\nfootnote_markers [\"1)\"].\n\n4. transitional_flowrate_q2, m³/h, min:\nFL5024: 0.32, 0.51, 0.80, 1.28, 2.02, 3.20, 5.12, 8.00, 12.80\nFL5044: 0.64, 2.50, 2.50, 2.56, 4.00, 6.40\n\n5. minimum_flowrate_q1, m³/h, min:\nFL5024: 0.20, 0.32, 0.50, 0.80, 1.26, 2.00, 3.20, 5.00, 8.00\nFL5044: 0.40, 1.58, 1.59, 1.60, 2.50, 4.00\n\n6. ratio_q3_q1, max, footnote 2):\nFL5024: 50 (DN32), 50 (40), 50 (50), 50 (65), 40 (80), 50 (100), 50 (125), 50 (150), 50 (200)\nFL5044: 100 (65), 40 (80), 63 (100), 100 (125), 100 (150), 100 (200)\nEach {min: null, max: X}, footnote [\"2)\"].\n\n7. resolution_of_indicating_device, L:\nFL5024: 1 (32), 1 (40), 1 (50), 1 (65), 10 (80), 10 (100), 10 (125), 10 (150), 10 (200)\nFL5044: 1 (65), 10 (80), 10 (100), 10 (125), 10 (150), 10 (200)\n\n8. resolution_of_rapid_testing_device, pulse/L:\nFL5024: 1.6, 1.6, 0.64, 0.32, 0.32 (DN32-80); 0.16, 0.16, 0.16, 0.064 (DN100-200)\nFL5044: 0.32 (65), 0.32 (80), 0.16 (100), 0.16 (125), 0.16 (150), 0.064 (200)\n\n9. flow_profile_sensitivity_class:\nFL5024: \"U10S D5\" (32), \"U10 D5\" (40,50,65,80,100,125,150,200)\nFL5044: \"U5 D3\" all.\n\n10. length, mm:\nFL5024: 260 (32), 300 (40), 300 (50), 300 (65), 350 (80), 350 (100), 350 (125), 350 (150), 350 (200)\nFL5044: 300 (65), 350 (80,100,125,150,200)\n\n11. indicating_range, m³:\nFL5024: {99999, 999999} for DN32-80; {999999, 9999999} for DN100-200\nFL5044: {999999, 9999999} all.\n\nThat's 11 model_level attributes. Good.\n\nmatrix_tables: three tables:\n\nTable 1: name \"Ultrasound Water Meter FLOMIC FL5024 – DN 32 to DN 80\"\ncolumns: [\"model\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"ratio_q4_q3\", \"orientation_limitation\", \"accuracy_class\", \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\", \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\", \"temperature_class\", \"water_pressure_class\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"indicating_range\", \"resolution_of_indicating_device\", \"resolution_of_rapid_testing_device\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"length\", \"connection_type\", \"environmental_class\", \"electromagnetic_class\", \"software_version\", \"wm_checksum\", \"power_supply_voltage\", \"battery_capacity\", \"battery_effective_life\"]\n\nHmm, that's very wide. The matrix_tables are meant to preserve multi-dim tables. The tables here are attribute × DN with many constant columns. Since model_level already captures varying attributes, maybe matrix_tables should keep the tables as-is for fidelity. I'll include them with full columns. column_units array too (schema example shows column_units). The schema yaml doesn't list column_units explicitly in the layered schema but the example includes it. The yaml: matrix_tables: name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]. The example JSON includes column_units. I'll include column_units since the example demonstrates it.\n\nRows keyed by DN: row objects like {\"nominal_diameter\": 32, \"overload_flowrate_q4\": \"≤ 12.5\", ...}. Should values in matrix_tables be structured objects? The strict rule says \"Every characteristic value MUST be an object\" — that applies to characteristics. For matrix_tables rows, the example shows plain numbers (qmax: 4.0). So plain values with units in column_units. I'll use numbers where possible, e.g. overload_flowrate_q4: 12.5 (the ≤ implied by column name? no...). To preserve ≤/≥ info, I could name columns q4_max, q3_max, q2_min, q1_min. In the R137 example, columns qmax/qt/qmin encode direction. I'll do similar: columns: nominal_diameter, q4_max, q3_max, q2_min, q1_min, ratio_q3_q1_max, ratio_q2_q1, ratio_q4_q3, ... Hmm but then model_level attribute names differ. Keep attribute names consistent-ish: I'll use columns: \"nominal_diameter\", \"q4_max\", \"q3_max\", \"q2_min\", \"q1_min\", \"q3_q1_ratio_max\", \"q2_q1_ratio\", \"q4_q3_ratio\", \"orientation\", \"accuracy_class\", \"mpe_lower_zone_percent\", \"mpe_upper_zone_percent\", \"temperature_class\", \"pressure_class\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"indicating_range_m3\"... Actually units go in column_units, so no unit suffixes.\n\nLet me define columns: [\"model\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"overload_flowrate_q4_max\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3_max\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2_min\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1_min\", \"ratio_q3_q1_max\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"ratio_q4_q3\", \"orientation_limitation\", \"accuracy_class\", \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\", \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\", \"temperature_class\", \"water_pressure_class\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"indicating_range_max\", \"resolution_of_indicating_device\", \"resolution_of_rapid_testing_device\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"length\", \"connection_type\", \"environmental_class\", \"electromagnetic_class\", \"software_version\", \"wm_checksum\", \"power_supply_voltage\", \"battery_capacity\", \"battery_effective_life\"]\n\ncolumn_units: [null, \"mm\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, null, null, null, null, \"%\", \"%\", null, null, null, \"m³\", \"m³\", \"L\", \"pulse/L\", null, \"mm\", null, null, null, null, null, \"V\", \"Ah\", \"years\"]\n\nValues in rows: numbers for numerics (12.5 etc. — the ≤ captured by \"_max\" suffix), strings for classes.\n\nRows for table 1 (FL5024 DN32-80):\n- model: \"FL5024-DN32\", nominal_diameter 32, q4_max 12.5, q3_max 10, q2_min 0.32, q1_min 0.20, ratio_q3_q1_max 50, ratio_q2_q1 1.6, ratio_q4_q3 1.25, orientation \"arbitrary\", accuracy_class 2, mpe_lower 5, mpe_upper 2, temp \"T30\", pressure \"MAP 16\", ΔP \"ΔP25\", indicating 99999–999999, res_ind 1, res_rapid 1.6, flow \"U10S D5\", length 260, conn \"standard flange\", env \"B\", emc \"E1 and E2\", sw \"M16V400-FL50X4\", checksum \"B543DAA9\", volt 3.6, cap 19, life 8.\n\nTable 2 (FL5024 DN100-200): 4 rows.\nTable 3 (FL5044 DN65-200): 6 rows.\n\nFootnotes for each table: the two footnotes. matrix_tables footnotes[]: list of the texts? Schema: footnotes[]. I'll include [{marker: \"1)\", text: \"...\"}, {marker: \"2)\", text: \"...\"}] or just strings. I'll use objects with marker/text for clarity. Top-level footnotes[] uses marker/text objects. For matrix_tables footnotes, I'll mirror.\n\nHmm, maybe simpler: matrix_tables footnotes: [\"1) The value of Q3...\", \"2) The ratio Q3/Q1...\"]. I'll use objects consistent with top-level.\n\nActually, to reduce bloat, maybe include column_units and rows fully — it's long but fine.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2006\n- amendment: null (omit)\n- scheme: \"A\"? The recommendation scheme... The schema: recommendation: id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]. Wait — scheme appears both at certificate level and recommendation level? Looking at schema: certificate.scheme (A|B) and recommendation.scheme. Hmm, the layered schema shows scheme under certificate only, and recommendation has \"scheme\" too: `recommendation: id (canonical...), edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]`. Yes it lists scheme there. I'll set scheme \"A\" at certificate level. For recommendation.scheme... maybe omit to avoid duplication confusion. Actually the schema explicitly lists it. It probably refers to the same OIML-CS scheme. I'll set both to \"A\"? Hmm. Risk of fabrication. The cert is a standard OIML Certificate of Conformity (pre-MAA), Scheme A. I'll set certificate.scheme: \"A\" and omit recommendation.scheme. Hmm, wait — maybe I should reconsider: is there an MAA for R49? R49 MAA exists (2017?). This cert is from 2012, before OIML-CS was introduced (2017-ish). The \"scheme A\" designation is retroactive but standard practice in these extraction tasks: default \"A\". I'll go with \"A\".\n\n- accuracy_classes: [2]\n\ntest_reports[]:\n- id: \"6015-PT-P0008-10\", pages: 39, role: \"test_report\", date: not present. Include note \"plus annexes\"? pages: 39. Role: it's the \"associated Test report\" → \"test_report\".\n\nrevision_history: not present → omit.\n\nfootnotes[]:\n- {marker: \"1)\", text: \"The value of Q3 shall be chosen from the R5 line of ISO 3:1973.\"}\n- {marker: \"2)\", text: \"The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen from the R10 line from ISO 3:1973 and this value shall be higher than 10.\"}\n\ndate_issued: 13 April 2012 → \"2012-04-13\".\n\ncertificate.number: \"R49/2006-CZ-12.01\".\n\nNow the description for certified_type: \"The water meters are intended for metering cold potable water. The water meters consist of flow sensor and of measuring transducer with the electronic calculator. The water meters are based on the ultrasonic principle. The flow sensor and the transducer is compact version, powered by battery. The body of flow sensor is made by shaped casting from material called ductile iron with standard flanges and installed by ultrasonic sensors which count is either one pair for single-beam model (type FL5024) or two pairs for double-beam model (type FL5044)...\"\n\nI'll include a trimmed version.\n\nModel attributes: FL5024 models: {\"beam_configuration\": \"single-beam (one pair of ultrasonic sensors)\"}; FL5044: \"double-beam (two pairs)\". For FL5024-DN32: {\"flow_straightener\": \"cross type flow straightener required\"}.\n\nNow the model IDs. Use \"FL5024-DN32\" style? Or \"FLOMIC FL5024 DN32\"? I'll use \"FL5024-DN32\" etc. Labels: \"FLOMIC FL5024, DN 32\".\n\nNow, per rules, model_level values objects: {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ..., \"footnote_markers\": [...]}. Wait, in the R60 example, values entries have {\"model\", \"value\"} only, with unit at attribute level. In rule 5 config values: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. For model_level, the schema yaml: model_level[]: attribute, unit, values[]: {model, value, footnote_markers[]}. So unit_symbol at attribute level (they use \"unit\" in yaml, \"unit_symbol\" in example). The R137 example uses \"unit_symbol\". I'll use unit_symbol at the attribute level, and values entries contain model, value, and footnote_markers where applicable.\n\nHmm, wait — rule 2 says every characteristic value MUST be an object with value/unit_symbol/footnote_markers shape. But the matrix examples contradict: {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}. The explicit examples take precedence for model_level matrices. I'll follow the matrix example format: attribute-level unit_symbol, value-level footnote_markers.\n\nValue shape for one-sided bounds: {min: null, max: 12.5} for Q4 ≤. The rules say open-ended ranges use null for missing bound. Q4 values are upper bounds: value {min: null, max: 12.5}. Q1/Q2 lower bounds: {min: 0.20, max: null}. Ratio Q3/Q1 ≤ 50: {min: null, max: 50}.\n\nHmm, but does that overcomplicate? The example for ≤2.5m/s: value {min: null, max: 2.5}. Yes, follow that.\n\nIndicating range: {min: 99999, max: 999999}, unit m³.\n\nNow let me also double check Q2 for FL5044 DN80: \"≥ 2.50\" — yes, table says DN65: 0.64, DN80: 2.50, DN100: 2.50, DN125: 2.56, DN150: 4.00, DN200: 6.40. And Q1: 0.40, 1.58, 1.59, 1.60, 2.50, 4.00. OK.\n\nFL5024 page 4 resolution rapid testing: 0.16, 0.16, 0.16, 0.064 for DN100,125,150,200.\n\nLength page 4: 350 all.\n\nFlow profile page 4: U10 D5 all.\n\nNow indicate column order and rows.\n\nLet me also decide: include \"component\" entries. I'll include them but keep characteristics simple:\n\ncomponents:\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"flow_sensor\",\n    \"type_designations\": [],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"ultrasonic\"},\n      \"body_material\": {\"value\": \"ductile iron (shaped casting)\"},\n      \"connection\": {\"value\": \"standard flange\"},\n      \"sensors\": {\"value\": \"one pair (single-beam, FL5024) or two pairs (double-beam, FL5044) of ultrasonic sensors\"}\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"measuring_transducer_with_calculator\",\n    \"type_designations\": [],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"housing\": {\"value\": \"aluminium box with plastic lid and transparent sight hole for LCD display\"},\n      \"display\": {\"value\": \"LCD\"},\n      \"communication_interfaces\": {\"value\": \"RS232, impulse output, current output, optical probe\"},\n      \"max_communication_cable_length\": {\"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": \"m\"},\n      \"power_supply\": {\"value\": \"battery 3.6 V / 19 Ah\"}\n    }\n  }\n]\n\nHmm, is including empty type_designations OK? I'll omit the key when empty... but then structure inconsistent. I'll just include empty arrays. Actually rule 8: don't fabricate — empty array isn't fabrication. Fine.\n\nShould \"connection\" be in flow sensor characteristics — it's in type_level as connection_type. Slight redundancy but OK. Maybe drop from component. Keep minimal: measuring_principle, body_material, sensor pairs. I'll simplify.\n\nNow the certificate scheme question again: \"scheme (A|B)\". I'll use \"A\".\n\nproject_number: not present — omit.\n\noiml_issuer_id: not present — omit at both levels.\n\nLet me now also think about \"person_title\": \"The Issuing Authority\" for Jan Kalandra. And Pavel Klenovský as CIML member — no schema slot; skip.\n\nNow build matrix_tables. I need to decide whether to include all constant columns — that's a lot of repetition. The schema wants preservation of multi-dim tables. I'll include them fully; it's mechanical.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the size. 3 tables × ~30 columns × rows (5+4+6). It's verbose but that's fine.\n\nAlternatively, I could give matrix_tables with only the varying columns to reduce redundancy, and note description \"constant attributes listed in characteristics.type_level\". Hmm. The R137 example includes all columns including constant pmax. I'll include everything for fidelity.\n\nWait, one more consideration: for table 2 (page 4), there is no header saying FL5024 — but it's clearly continuation (page 3 header \"Ultrasound Water Meter FLOMIC FL5024\", \"Further characteristics see page 3, 4 and 5\"). I'll name it accordingly and note in description.\n\nNow write the model_level entries. Model IDs list:\n\nFL5024-DN32, FL5024-DN40, FL5024-DN50, FL5024-DN65, FL5024-DN80, FL5024-DN100, FL5024-DN125, FL5024-DN150, FL5024-DN200, FL5044-DN65, FL5044-DN80, FL5044-DN100, FL5044-DN125, FL5044-DN150, FL5044-DN200.\n\nLet me write out model_level arrays:\n\nnominal_diameter (mm):\n32,40,50,65,80,100,125,150,200 for FL5024 respective; 65,80,100,125,150,200 for FL5044.\n\noverload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h) — max:\nFL5024: 12.5, 20.0, 31.3, 50.0, 78.8, 125.0, 200.0, 312.5, 500.0\nFL5044: 50, 79, 125, 200, 313, 500\n\npermanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h) — max, fn 1:\nFL5024: 10, 16, 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400\nFL5044: 40, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400\n\ntransitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h) — min:\nFL5024: 0.32, 0.51, 0.80, 1.28, 2.02, 3.20, 5.12, 8.00, 12.80\nFL5044: 0.64, 2.50, 2.50, 2.56, 4.00, 6.40\n\nminimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h) — min:\nFL5024: 0.20, 0.32, 0.50, 0.80, 1.26, 2.00, 3.20, 5.00, 8.00\nFL5044: 0.40, 1.58, 1.59, 1.60, 2.50, 4.00\n\nratio_q3_q1 (max, fn 2):\nFL5024: 50,50,50,50,40,50,50,50,50\nFL5044: 100,40,63,100,100,100\n\nresolution_of_indicating_device (L):\nFL5024: 1,1,1,1,10,10,10,10,10\nFL5044: 1,10,10,10,10,10\n\nresolution_of_rapid_testing_device (pulse/L):\nFL5024: 1.6,1.6,0.64,0.32,0.32,0.16,0.16,0.16,0.064\nFL5044: 0.32,0.32,0.16,0.16,0.16,0.064\n\nflow_profile_sensitivity_class:\nFL5024: U10S D5 (32); U10 D5 (rest)\nFL5044: U5 D3\n\nlength (mm):\nFL5024: 260,300,300,300,350,350,350,350,350\nFL5044: 300,350,350,350,350,350\n\nindicating_range (m³):\nFL5024: {99999,999999} ×5 (DN32-80), then {999999,9999999} ×4\nFL5044: {999999,9999999} ×6\n\nNote FL5044 DN65: resolution 1 L, indicating range 999999–9999999 — matches table 3.\n\nNow assemble the JSON. Be careful with valid JSON, no trailing commas.\n\nAlso top-level \"certificate.page_total\": 5.\n\ndate format: \"2012-04-13\".\n\nType designations: [\"FLOMIC FL5024\", \"FLOMIC FL5044\"]. Also \"FL50X4\" appears in software version — not a type designation per se. Skip.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\".\n\nmodel_family: family_name null. Models with label and attributes.\n\nFor attributes in models entries: beam_configuration values: \"single-beam\" / \"double-beam\". FL5024-DN32 additionally flow_straightener: \"cross type (required)\".\n\ncertified_type.description: I'll write concise: \"Ultrasound water meter for cold potable water, based on the ultrasonic principle, consisting of a flow sensor and a measuring transducer with electronic calculator in compact version, battery powered. Flow sensor body of ductile iron shaped casting with standard flanges, fitted with one pair of ultrasonic sensors for single-beam model FL5024 or two pairs for double-beam model FL5044. Measuring transducer in aluminium housing with plastic lid, LCD display, button, pressure-equalization valve, optical probe communication plate and connectors for RS232, impulse output and current output. Unused connectors must be blind; maximum communication cable length 2 m. FL5024 with DN32 must be installed with a cross-type flow straightener.\"\n\nGood.\n\nNow matrix_tables details.\n\nTable 1 name: \"Ultrasound Water Meter FLOMIC FL5024 – DN 32 to DN 80\"\ndescription: \"Characteristics of FLOMIC FL5024 for nominal diameters DN 32–80 (certificate page 3).\"\ncolumns and column_units:\n[\"model\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"overload_flowrate_q4_max\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3_max\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2_min\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1_min\", \"ratio_q3_q1_max\", \"ratio_q2_q1\", \"ratio_q4_q3\", \"orientation_limitation\", \"accuracy_class\", \"mpe_lower_flowrate_zone\", \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\", \"temperature_class\", \"water_pressure_class\", \"pressure_loss_class\", \"indicating_range_min\", \"indicating_range_max\", \"resolution_of_indicating_device\", \"resolution_of_rapid_testing_device\", \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\", \"length\", \"connection_type\", \"environmental_class\", \"electromagnetic_class\", \"software_version\", \"wm_checksum\", \"power_supply_voltage\", \"battery_capacity\", \"battery_effective_life\"]\nunits: [null,\"mm\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",\"m³/h\",null,null,null,null,null,\"%\",\"%\",null,null,null,\"m³\",\"m³\",\"L\",\"pulse/L\",null,\"mm\",null,null,null,null,null,\"V\",\"Ah\",\"years\"]\n\nmpe_upper_flowrate_zone row value: 2 (condition ≤30°C noted in description/footnotes). I'll add table footnote 3? No — don't fabricate footnote numbering. I'll note in table description: \"MPEu ±2 % applies for water temperature ≤ 30 °C.\" Good.\n\nRows table1:\nrow DN32: model \"FL5024-DN32\", 32, 12.5, 10, 0.32, 0.20, 50, 1.6, 1.25, \"arbitrary\", 2, 5, 2, \"T30\", \"MAP 16\", \"ΔP25\", 99999, 999999, 1, 1.6, \"U10S D5\", 260, \"standard flange\", \"B\", \"E1 and E2\", \"M16V400-FL50X4\", \"B543DAA9\", 3.6, 19, 8\nrow DN40: ..., 40, 20.0, 16, 0.51, 0.32, 50, ..., 1, 1.6, \"U10 D5\", 300, ...\nrow DN50: 50, 31.3, 25, 0.80, 0.50, 50, ..., 1, 0.64, \"U10 D5\", 300\nrow DN65: 65, 50.0, 40, 1.28, 0.80, 50, ..., 1, 0.32, \"U10 D5\", 300\nrow DN80: 80, 78.8, 63, 2.02, 1.26, 40, ..., 10, 0.32, \"U10 D5\", 350\n\nFootnotes for tables 1-3: the two certificate footnotes. Include footnote markers on q3 and ratio columns implicitly? matrix rows are plain; footnotes listed separately. I'll include footnotes arrays with both markers.\n\nTable 2 name: \"Ultrasound Water Meter FLOMIC FL5024 – DN 100 to DN 200\" (continuation, page 4).\nrows:\nDN100: 100, 125.0, 100, 3.20, 2.00, 50, 1.6, 1.25, arbitrary, 2, 5, 2, T30, MAP 16, ΔP25, 999999, 9999999, 10, 0.16, U10 D5, 350, standard flange, B, E1 and E2, M16V400-FL50X4, B543DAA9, 3.6, 19, 8\nDN125: 125, 200.0, 160, 5.12, 3.20, 50, ..., 10, 0.16, U10 D5, 350\nDN150: 150, 312.5, 250, 8.00, 5.00, 50, ..., 10, 0.16, U10 D5, 350\nDN200: 200, 500.0, 400, 12.80, 8.00, 50, ..., 10, 0.064, U10 D5, 350\n\nTable 3 name: \"Ultrasound Water Meter FLOMIC FL5044 – DN 65 to DN 200\" (page 5).\nrows:\nDN65: 65, 50, 40, 0.64, 0.40, 100, 1.6, 1.25, arbitrary, 2, 5, 2, T30, MAP 16, ΔP25, 999999, 9999999, 1, 0.32, U5 D3, 300, standard flange, B, E1 and E2, M16V400-FL50X4, B543DAA9, 3.6, 19, 8\nDN80: 80, 79, 63, 2.50, 1.58, 40, ..., 10, 0.32, U5 D3, 350\nDN100: 100, 125, 100, 2.50, 1.59, 63, ..., 10, 0.16, U5 D3, 350\nDN125: 125, 200, 160, 2.56, 1.60, 100, ..., 10, 0.16, U5 D3, 350\nDN150: 150, 313, 250, 4.00, 2.50, 100, ..., 10, 0.16, U5 D3, 350\nDN200: 200, 500, 400, 6.40, 4.00, 100, ..., 10, 0.064, U5 D3, 350\n\nOK. That's large but fine.\n\nWait — top-level footnotes text: \"The value of Q3 shall be chosen from the R5 line of ISO 3:1973.\" Use \"Q3\" plain (no LaTeX). Good.\n\ntest_reports: [{\"id\": \"6015-PT-P0008-10\", \"date\": null?, \"pages\": 39, \"role\": \"test_report\"}]. Omit date since not present. Rule: omit missing fields. I'll omit date key.\n\nAlso certificate.date_issued: \"2012-04-13\".\n\nAlso should I include the certificate \"scheme\": \"A\". And recommendation: {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2006, \"accuracy_classes\": [2], \"scheme\": \"A\"}. Hmm, I'll include scheme in recommendation as \"A\" too? The yaml lists it. Both is redundant but schema-compliant. I'll include \"A\" in both? Let me just include in certificate and omit in recommendation to avoid asserting something twice... Actually schema explicitly lists recommendation.scheme, so include it. Fine, both \"A\".\n\nHmm, wait. Actually let me reconsider: maybe \"scheme\" in recommendation refers to something else... In OIML-CS extraction context, certificate scheme A (basic) vs B (MAA). It's document-level mostly. The layered schema yaml:\n\n```\nrecommendation:\n  id (canonical, e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\"), edition (int), amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]\n```\n\nOK it includes scheme. I'll put \"A\".\n\nNow components characteristics values: rule 2 structured values with value/unit_symbol shape — I'll wrap: {\"value\": \"ductile iron\", \"unit_symbol\": null}? For strings, unit_symbol null. The shape requires unit_symbol and optionally unit_id, footnote_markers. To be safe, I'll give {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": null} for component characteristics. Actually for type_level too! Rule 2 says EVERY characteristic value must be an object with that shape. But then the matrix examples for model_level show raw values... The conflict again. Let me follow this interpretation:\n\n- type_level: structured objects {value, unit_symbol, footnote_markers?} (rule 2 explicitly).\n- model_level/config_level: follow matrix examples (attribute-level unit, value-level raw).\n- components characteristics: structured objects.\n\nFor type_level objects, should I include unit_id? \"Leave unit_id: null unless certain\". Should I include \"unit_id\": null everywhere? That's noisy. The shape shows unit_id key; I'll include it only when I set it, or always include as null? I'll include unit_id: null consistently in type_level to match the shape. Hmm, \"MUST be an object with this shape\" — I'll include unit_id: null in type_level values. For components too maybe. Keep consistent.\n\nActually for cleanliness I'll include unit_id: null in type_level and component values. In model_level matrix values, example doesn't include unit_id; follow example.\n\nDimensionless values like ratio_q2_q1: {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}.\n\nStrings: orientation_limitation: {\"value\": \"arbitrary\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}.\n\naccuracy_class: {\"value\": 2, ...}.\n\nmpe_lower_flowrate_zone: {\"value\": {\"min\": -5, \"max\": 5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\", \"unit_id\": null}.\n\nconfig_level mpe_upper_flowrate_zone: {\"attribute\": \"mpe_upper_flowrate_zone\", \"axis\": \"water_temperature\", \"values\": [{\"condition\": \"≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": 2, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}]}. Sign: ±2 → value {min:-2, max:2}? For config values, keep {min:-2, max:2}. Hmm the example rule 5: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. value can be object. I'll use {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}.\n\ntemperature_class \"T30\": value \"T30\".\nwater_pressure_class: \"MAP 16\" — value \"MAP16\"? Keep \"MAP 16\" as-is.\npressure_loss_class: \"ΔP25\". The markdown shows \"<i>ΔP</i> 25\". Value \"ΔP25\". Fine.\nconnection_type: \"standard flange\".\nenvironmental_class: \"B\".\nelectromagnetic_class: \"E1 and E2\". Maybe \"E1, E2\"? Keep as in doc: \"E1 and E2\".\nsoftware_version: \"M16V400 – FL50X4\" → I'll write \"M16V400 - FL50X4\" (avoid en dash issues? JSON handles Unicode fine; but keep simple hyphen).\nwm_checksum: \"B543DAA9\".\npower_supply_voltage: 3.6 V.\nbattery_capacity: 19 Ah.\nbattery_effective_life: 8 years → unit_symbol \"years\" (or \"a\"). Use \"years\".\n\nAlso battery is the power supply — fine.\n\nLet me now also decide model attribute for beam configuration string: FL5024: \"single-beam\"; FL5044: \"double-beam\".\n\nNow, one subtlety: In table 1, ratio_q3_q1 for DN80 is ≤40; page 3 row: colspan 4 (DN32,40,50,65) ≤50, then ≤40 for DN80. Yes.\n\nAlso table 1 (page 3) flow profile: DN32 \"U10S D5\", colspan 4 \"U10 D5\" for DN40-80. Yes.\n\nResolution indicating device page 3: colspan 4 → 1 for DN32-65; DN80 → 10. Yes.\n\nEverything checks.\n\nNow, let me think about whether to include \"certificate.project_number\" — not present, omit. \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\".\n\nLet me draft final JSON.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2006-CZ-12.01\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 5,\n    \"member_state\"